  "text" : "This model is 99.5% accurate, but it's never caught a single attack.\n10,000 requests come in, but only 50 are attacks.\nHere's a real model. It catches 40 of the 50 attacks,\nbut its accuracy goes down to 99.3%.\nSo we look at the confusion matrix instead.\nA true positive is \"I said there was an attack, and it was genuinely an attack.\"\nA false positive is \"The model predicted attack, but it was a real user.\"\nA false negative, \"I said user, but it was an attack.\"\nIn a true negative, I said user and it was a user.\nNow look where the accuracy comes from.\nAlmost all of it is true negatives.\nThese are the easy ones.\nWhen positives are rare,\nwe don't want to read the accuracy.\nWe want to look at the confusion matrix."
